  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "After adjusting for employment pattern, higher night-time phone use remains associated with higher gambling stake.",
      "verdict": "supported",
      "evidence": [
        4,
        5,
        6
      ],
      "reason": "The GLM on the bets dataset shows significant positive coefficients for night_use_band after including employment terms (step6), and the mean stake per bet increases with night_use_band within each employment group (step5)."
    },
    {
      "text": "The observed association can be considered plausibly causal.",
      "verdict": "not_answerable",
      "evidence": [],
      "reason": "Causality requires temporal ordering and control for unmeasured confounders, which cannot be established from the available aggregate data."
    }
  ],
  "verdict": "supported",
  "notes": "The analysis relies on event-level (bets) aggregates; person-month panel models adjusting for employment were not releasable due to disclosure constraints. Residual confounding and lack of longitudinal timing limit causal interpretation.",
  "budget": 20,
  "budget_spent": 7,
  "stopped_because": "concluded",
  "narrative": "After adjusting for employment pattern, higher night\u2011time phone use remains associated with higher gambling stakes. In a model that includes only night\u2011use band, the intercept is 9.43\u202fGBP and the coefficients for occasional, regular and heavy use are 3.48, 8.13 and 39.23\u202fGBP respectively (step\u202f4). When employment pattern is added, the intercept becomes 19.47\u202fGBP and the night\u2011use coefficients stay positive: occasional\u202f0.41\u202fGBP, regular\u202f1.13\u202fGBP and heavy\u202f7.98\u202fGBP, while shift workers have an additional 5.05\u202fGBP, students \u20130.87\u202fGBP and those not working 0.21\u202fGBP (step\u202f6). Mean stake per bet also rises with heavier night\u2011use within each employment group (e.g., heavy shift workers 36.04\u202fGBP vs. occasional day workers 20.34\u202fGBP) (step\u202f5).  \n\nThe data do not allow a causal interpretation. Because the analysis relies on aggregate bet\u2011level information and person\u2011month panel models adjusting for employment were not releasable due to disclosure constraints, temporal ordering and control for unmeasured confounders cannot be established; therefore the question of whether the observed association is plausibly causal is not answerable.",
  "unsupported_figures": [
    "0.87"
  ]
}